const PASS_BEES="Since the mid-2000s, beekeepers have reported puzzling losses of honeybee colonies, a phenomenon often called colony collapse disorder. Scientists have proposed several causes, including pesticides, parasites, and habitat loss. Recent studies point to a combination of factors rather than a single culprit. For example, the varroa mite weakens bees by feeding on them, while certain pesticides may make bees more vulnerable to infection. In addition, large-scale agriculture reduces the variety of flowering plants, leaving bees with a less nutritious diet. Researchers now believe these stressors interact: a bee colony already weakened by poor nutrition may be more easily overwhelmed by disease. This understanding has important implications, because honeybees pollinate many of the crops humans rely on for food. Protecting bees therefore requires addressing multiple threats at once rather than focusing on any single cause.";
const PASS_GARDEN="Elena stood at the edge of the garden, where the tomato plants her grandmother had tended for decades now sprawled untended. She had promised herself she would restore them, but every weekend brought another excuse. That morning, however, the sight of a single ripe tomato, hidden beneath a drooping leaf, stopped her. It was small and lopsided, nothing like the plump fruit she remembered from childhood summers. Yet something about it felt like an invitation. She knelt in the dirt, pulled on the old gardening gloves still hanging by the fence, and began, one weed at a time, to clear the soil. By afternoon, her hands were sore and her knees stiff, but the garden looked less like a memory and more like a beginning.";
const PASS_ECON="Economists once assumed that people make financial decisions by carefully weighing costs and benefits. Behavioral economists have challenged this view, showing that people are influenced by mental shortcuts, or heuristics. One well-known example is the “anchoring effect,” in which an initial number, even an arbitrary one, influences later judgments. In a classic experiment, participants who were first shown a high number guessed higher values for unrelated questions than those shown a low number. Such findings matter beyond the laboratory: retailers, for instance, may display an inflated “original” price so that the sale price seems like a bargain. Recognizing these biases, some policymakers now advocate “nudges”—small changes to how choices are presented—to help people make better decisions without restricting their freedom.";
const PASS_HISTORY="In 1848, a small group of reformers gathered in Seneca Falls, New York, to demand equal rights for women. The convention produced a Declaration of Sentiments, modeled on the Declaration of Independence, which declared that “all men and women are created equal.” The document listed grievances against laws that denied women the right to vote, to own property, and to receive a full education. At the time, many Americans dismissed these demands as radical, and the right to vote was not secured for women nationwide until the ratification of the Nineteenth Amendment in 1920. Yet historians note that the Seneca Falls convention did more than issue a list of complaints: it created a movement. By borrowing the language of the nation's founding, its organizers argued that women's rights were not a departure from American ideals but their fulfillment.";
const PASS_BIRDS="A team of biologists studying songbirds noticed that males in noisy urban areas sing at higher pitches than those in quiet forests. To test whether this difference was an adaptation, the researchers recorded the same species in both environments and played the recordings back in each. They found that urban females responded more strongly to the higher-pitched songs, while forest females preferred lower-pitched songs. Further analysis suggested that low-frequency traffic noise masks the lower notes of a song, making them harder to hear. Over generations, males that sang slightly higher may therefore have gained an advantage in the city. The study is one of several indicating that human-generated noise can drive evolutionary change, not merely disrupt animal behavior.";
const PASS_LETTER="He had spent the entire journey composing the words he would say, but at the threshold the sentences scattered like startled birds. For thirty years he had carried the letter, unopened, in the drawer where he kept the few things he could not bring himself to discard. It was not the contents he feared, he told himself, but the end of a question he had come to prefer over any answer. Now, with his hand raised to knock, he understood that some doors are kept closed not to shut others out, but to preserve the version of ourselves that lives behind them. He lowered his hand, turned, and walked back into the long afternoon, the unopened letter still waiting, patient as stone.";
const PASS_SLEEP="Neuroscientists have long debated why sleep is necessary, since an animal that is asleep cannot hunt, eat, or defend itself. One influential theory holds that sleep allows the brain to clear away metabolic waste products that build up during waking hours. Supporting this idea, researchers found that the space between brain cells expands during sleep, increasing the flow of fluid that flushes out waste. Other scientists caution that the evidence is not yet conclusive: the brain may also need sleep to consolidate memories, regulate hormones, or repair cells. If the waste-clearance theory is correct, it would help explain why nearly every animal sleeps despite the apparent risks, and why prolonged sleep deprivation impairs thinking so severely.";
const PASS_TRADE="Economists distinguish between absolute and comparative advantage to explain why countries trade. A country has an absolute advantage when it can produce a good using fewer resources than another country. But even a country that is less efficient at producing everything can still benefit from trade if it has a comparative advantage—the ability to produce a good at a lower opportunity cost. Opportunity cost is what must be given up to produce one more unit of something. By specializing in the goods for which their opportunity costs are lowest and trading for the rest, countries can, in principle, consume more than they could produce on their own. This insight, developed by economist David Ricardo in the early nineteenth century, remains a cornerstone of international trade theory.";

const BANK = {
  math: [
    {id:"m1",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"3x + 7 = 22. What is the value of x?",choices:["4","5","6","7"],ans:1,exp:"To isolate x, first subtract 7 from both sides: 3x = 15. Then divide both sides by 3 to get x = 5. Undo the “+7,” then the “×3.”"},
    {id:"m2",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"If five more than twice a number is 21, what is the number?",choices:["6","7","8","9"],ans:2,exp:"“Twice a number” is 2x, and “five more than” that is 2x + 5. Set 2x + 5 = 21, subtract 5 to get 2x = 16, then divide by 2: x = 8."},
    {id:"m3",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"What is 20% of 150?",choices:["20","25","30","35"],ans:2,exp:"“Percent” means “out of 100,” so 20% = 0.20. Multiply 150 × 0.20 = 30."},
    {id:"m4",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A recipe calls for 2 cups of flour for every 3 cups of sugar. How many cups of flour are needed for 9 cups of sugar?",choices:["4","6","8","12"],ans:1,exp:"Set up a proportion: 2/3 = x/9. Cross-multiply to get 3x = 18, so x = 6. The flour is always two-thirds as much as the sugar."},
    {id:"m5",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"If x ÷ 4 = 12, what is the value of x?",choices:["3","24","36","48"],ans:3,exp:"To undo “divide by 4,” multiply both sides by 4: x = 12 × 4 = 48."},
    {id:"m6",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"If f(x) = 2x − 3, what is f(7)?",choices:["9","10","11","14"],ans:2,exp:"Replace x with 7: f(7) = 2(7) − 3 = 14 − 3 = 11."},
    {id:"m7",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"What is the mean of 4, 8, 9, and 11?",choices:["7","8","9","11"],ans:1,exp:"Add the numbers: 4 + 8 + 9 + 11 = 32. Divide by how many numbers there are (4): 32 ÷ 4 = 8."},
    {id:"m8",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A car travels 240 miles in 4 hours at a constant speed. What is its speed in miles per hour?",choices:["50","55","60","65"],ans:2,exp:"Speed = distance ÷ time = 240 ÷ 4 = 60 miles per hour."},
    {id:"m9",diff:"easy",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Simplify 2³ × 2².",choices:["2⁵","2⁶","4⁵","4⁶"],ans:0,exp:"When you multiply powers with the same base, add the exponents: 2³ × 2² = 2³⁺² = 2⁵. Don't multiply the bases."},
    {id:"m10",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"What is the slope of the line through the points (0, 0) and (2, 6)?",choices:["1/3","2","3","6"],ans:2,exp:"Slope is rise over run: (6 − 0)/(2 − 0) = 6/2 = 3. The line goes up 3 for every 1 it moves right."},
    {id:"m11",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"Solve the inequality x + 5 > 12.",choices:["x > 5","x > 7","x < 7","x < 17"],ans:1,exp:"Subtract 5 from both sides: x > 7. The sign stays the same because you're not multiplying or dividing by a negative."},
    {id:"m12",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"At 5% simple annual interest, how much interest does $200 earn in one year?",choices:["$5","$10","$20","$50"],ans:1,exp:"Interest = principal × rate = 200 × 0.05 = $10."},
    {id:"m13",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"Solve the system:\nx + y = 10\nx − y = 4\nWhat is the value of x?",choices:["3","5","7","8"],ans:2,exp:"Add the two equations: (x+y) + (x−y) = 10 + 4 gives 2x = 14, so x = 7. Adding cancels out the y terms."},
    {id:"m14",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"What are the solutions to x² − 5x + 6 = 0?",choices:["1 and 6","2 and 3","−2 and −3","−1 and −6"],ans:1,exp:"Factor: (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0. Set each factor to zero: x = 2 or x = 3. The numbers multiply to 6 and add to 5."},
    {id:"m15",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A price rises from $80 to $100. What is the percent increase?",choices:["15%","20%","25%","30%"],ans:2,exp:"The change is 100 − 80 = 20. Divide by the original: 20 ÷ 80 = 0.25, or 25%."},
    {id:"m16",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"If f(x) = 3x − 4, for what value of x does f(x) = 20?",choices:["6","8","9","12"],ans:1,exp:"Set 3x − 4 = 20. Add 4 to get 3x = 24, then divide by 3: x = 8."},
    {id:"m17",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"If √(2x + 1) = 5, what is the value of x?",choices:["8","10","12","24"],ans:2,exp:"Square both sides to remove the radical: 2x + 1 = 25. Subtract 1 to get 2x = 24, so x = 12."},
    {id:"m18",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"If a/b = 2/3 and b = 18, what is the value of a?",choices:["6","9","12","27"],ans:2,exp:"Substitute b = 18: a/18 = 2/3. Multiply both sides by 18: a = 18 × (2/3) = 12."},
    {id:"m19",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Simplify (x³)².",choices:["x⁵","x⁶","x⁹","2x³"],ans:1,exp:"When raising a power to a power, multiply the exponents: (x³)² = x³ˣ² = x⁶."},
    {id:"m20",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"What is the slope of the line 2y = 6x + 4?",choices:["2","3","4","6"],ans:1,exp:"Put the line in y = mx + b form by dividing by 2: y = 3x + 2. The slope m is 3."},
    {id:"m21",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"What is the median of 3, 7, 2, 9, and 5?",choices:["3","5","7","9"],ans:1,exp:"Put the numbers in order: 2, 3, 5, 7, 9. The median is the middle value, 5."},
    {id:"m22",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"A delivery fee is $5 plus $2 per item. What is the total cost for 8 items?",choices:["$16","$18","$21","$26"],ans:2,exp:"The cost is 5 + 2(8) = 5 + 16 = $21. The $5 is a fixed fee, and each item adds $2."},
    {id:"m23",diff:"medium",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A triangle has base 10 and height 6. What is its area?",choices:["16","30","32","60"],ans:1,exp:"Area of a triangle = ½ × base × height = ½ × 10 × 6 = 30."},
    {id:"m24",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"If y is directly proportional to x, and y = 10 when x = 4, what is y when x = 10?",choices:["20","25","40","50"],ans:1,exp:"Direct proportion means y = kx. Find k = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5. Then y = 2.5 × 10 = 25."},
    {id:"m25",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"If g(x) = x² − 2x, what is g(3)?",choices:["0","3","6","9"],ans:1,exp:"Plug in 3: g(3) = 3² − 2(3) = 9 − 6 = 3."},
    {id:"m26",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Expand (2x + 3)(x − 1).",choices:["2x² + x − 3","2x² + 5x − 3","2x² − 5x + 3","2x² + x + 3"],ans:0,exp:"FOIL: 2x·x = 2x², 2x·(−1) = −2x, 3·x = 3x, 3·(−1) = −3. Combine the middle terms: −2x + 3x = x, so the result is 2x² + x − 3."},
    {id:"m27",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"What is the minimum value of f(x) = x² − 6x + 8?",choices:["−1","0","1","3"],ans:0,exp:"A quadratic opens upward, so its minimum is at the vertex. The vertex x-value is −b/2a = 6/2 = 3. Plug in: f(3) = 9 − 18 + 8 = −1."},
    {id:"m28",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"The graphs of y = x² and y = 2x + 3 intersect. What are the x-coordinates of the intersection points?",choices:["−1 and 3","−3 and 1","1 and 2","−1 and 1"],ans:0,exp:"Set the y-values equal: x² = 2x + 3. Rearrange to x² − 2x − 3 = 0, then factor: (x − 3)(x + 1) = 0. So x = 3 or x = −1."},
    {id:"m29",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"A quantity grows 10% per year. By what factor does it grow after 3 years?",choices:["1.1","1.3","1.331","3.3"],ans:2,exp:"Each year the quantity is multiplied by 1.10. Over three years: 1.10³ = 1.331. Don't just add the percentages."},
    {id:"m30",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Factor x² − 9.",choices:["(x − 3)²","(x + 3)²","(x − 3)(x + 3)","(x − 9)(x + 1)"],ans:2,exp:"This is a difference of squares: a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b). Here a = x and b = 3, so x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3)."},
    {id:"m31",diff:"hard",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A price decreases by 20%, then the new price increases by 25%. Compared with the original price, the final price is…",choices:["unchanged","5% lower","5% higher","10% higher"],ans:0,exp:"Multiply the two changes: 0.80 × 1.25 = 1.00. They cancel, so the price returns to its original value."},
    {id:"m32",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"If a + b = 8 and ab = 15, what is the value of a² + b²?",choices:["34","49","64","94"],ans:0,exp:"Use (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b². Plug in: 8² = a² + 2(15) + b², so 64 = a² + 30 + b². Subtract 30: a² + b² = 34."},
    {id:"m33",diff:"hard",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A circle has area 16π. What is its circumference?",choices:["4π","8π","16π","32π"],ans:1,exp:"Area = πr², so πr² = 16π, meaning r² = 16 and r = 4. Circumference = 2πr = 8π."},
    {id:"m34",diff:"hard",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"The mean of five numbers is 12. Four of the numbers are 10, 14, 11, and 13. What is the fifth number?",choices:["10","11","12","13"],ans:2,exp:"If the mean of five numbers is 12, their total is 5 × 12 = 60. The four known numbers add to 48, so the fifth is 60 − 48 = 12."},
    {id:"m35",diff:"hard",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"One pipe fills a tank in 6 hours and another fills it in 3 hours. Working together, how long do they take to fill the tank?",choices:["1.5 hours","2 hours","2.5 hours","4.5 hours"],ans:1,exp:"Rates: 1/6 and 1/3 of a tank per hour. Together they fill 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/2 tank per hour, so the whole tank takes 2 hours."},
    {id:"m36",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Solve |x − 3| = 7.",choices:["x = 10 only","x = −4 only","x = −4 or x = 10","x = 4 or x = −10"],ans:2,exp:"Absolute value means the inside is either 7 or −7. So x − 3 = 7 (x = 10) or x − 3 = −7 (x = −4)."},
    {id:"m37",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"If f(x) = 2ˣ, what is f(4) ÷ f(2)?",choices:["2","4","8","16"],ans:1,exp:"f(4) = 2⁴ = 16 and f(2) = 2² = 4. Divide: 16 ÷ 4 = 4."},
    {id:"m38",diff:"hard",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A right triangle has legs of length 3 and 4. What is the length of the hypotenuse?",choices:["5","6","7","√7"],ans:0,exp:"Use the Pythagorean theorem: a² + b² = c², so 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25 = c². Therefore c = √25 = 5."},
    {id:"m39",diff:"easy",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A rectangle has length 8 and width 5. What is its perimeter?",choices:["13","26","40","80"],ans:1,exp:"Perimeter = 2(length + width) = 2(8 + 5) = 26. Perimeter is the distance all the way around."},
    {id:"m40",diff:"easy",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"Two angles of a triangle measure 50° and 60°. What is the measure of the third angle?",choices:["50°","60°","70°","80°"],ans:2,exp:"The three angles of a triangle always add to 180°. So the third angle is 180 − 50 − 60 = 70°."},
    {id:"m41",diff:"easy",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"What is the area of a circle with radius 4?",choices:["4π","8π","16π","64π"],ans:2,exp:"Area = πr² = π(4²) = 16π. Square the radius, then multiply by π."},
    {id:"m42",diff:"medium",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A cylinder has radius 3 and height 4. What is its volume?",choices:["12π","24π","36π","48π"],ans:2,exp:"Volume of a cylinder = πr²h = π(3²)(4) = π(9)(4) = 36π."},
    {id:"m43",diff:"medium",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"In a right triangle, the side opposite angle X has length 8 and the hypotenuse has length 10. What is sin X?",choices:["0.6","0.8","1.25","0.75"],ans:1,exp:"Sine is opposite ÷ hypotenuse: sin X = 8/10 = 0.8. SOH-CAH-TOA: sine = opposite over hypotenuse."},
    {id:"m44",diff:"hard",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A circle is described by (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25. What is the center of the circle?",choices:["(3, −2)","(−3, 2)","(3, 2)","(−3, −2)"],ans:0,exp:"A circle in the form (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r² has center (h, k). Here h = 3 and k = −2, so the center is (3, −2)."},
    {id:"m45",diff:"hard",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A 12-foot ladder leans against a wall, making a 60° angle with the ground. How high up the wall does the ladder reach?",choices:["6 feet","6√3 feet","12 feet","24 feet"],ans:1,exp:"The height is the side opposite the 60° angle, so use sine: height = 12 × sin 60° = 12 × (√3/2) = 6√3 feet."},
    {id:"m46",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A bag contains 4 red, 3 blue, and 2 green marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability it is not green?",choices:["2/9","7/9","4/9","1/3"],ans:1,exp:"There are 9 marbles total and 2 are green, so 7 are not green. Probability = favorable/total = 7/9."},
    {id:"m47",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A scatterplot shows a strong positive linear relationship between two variables. Which correlation coefficient best matches the data?",choices:["0.9","0.1","−0.9","0.0"],ans:0,exp:"A correlation near +1 means a strong positive linear relationship. 0.9 is strong and positive; −0.9 would be strong and negative."}
  ],
  rw: [
    {id:"w1",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nThe committee ___ its final decision next week.",choices:["will announce","announcing","announce","announces"],ans:0,exp:"The sentence describes something happening “next week,” so it needs the future tense “will announce.” “Announcing” is missing a helping verb, and “announces” is present tense."},
    {id:"w2",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nEach of the students ___ a laptop for the class.",choices:["have","has","are having","having"],ans:1,exp:"“Each” is singular, so the verb must be singular: “has.” Ignore the plural “students” after “of” — the subject is “each.”"},
    {id:"w3",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Which sentence uses punctuation correctly?",choices:["Its a beautiful day outside.","It's a beautiful day outside.","Its' a beautiful day outside.","Its a beautiful day, outside."],ans:1,exp:"“It's” is the contraction for “it is.” “Its” (no apostrophe) is possessive, and “Its'” is never correct."},
    {id:"w4",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the word that best fits the sentence.\nThe new policy will have a significant ___ on students.",choices:["affect","effect","effective","affection"],ans:1,exp:"“Effect” is a noun meaning a result or influence, which is what this sentence needs. “Affect” is usually a verb, and “effective” is an adjective."},
    {id:"w5",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nNeither the coach nor the players ___ ready for the game.",choices:["was","were","is","be"],ans:1,exp:"With “neither/nor,” the verb agrees with the nearer subject, “players,” which is plural. So the plural verb “were” is correct."},
    {id:"w6",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nBetween you and ___, I think we should leave early.",choices:["I","me","myself","mine"],ans:1,exp:"After a preposition like “between,” use the object pronoun “me,” not the subject pronoun “I.”"},
    {id:"w7",diff:"easy",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the most concise and correct version.",choices:["The reason is because the train was delayed.","The reason is that the train was delayed.","The reason being the train was delayed.","Because the reason was that the train was delayed."],ans:1,exp:"“The reason is because” is redundant. “The reason is that the train was delayed” says the same thing cleanly."},
    {id:"w8",diff:"easy",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the best transition.\nShe studied hard. ___, she passed the exam.",choices:["Therefore","However","Meanwhile","Although"],ans:0,exp:"The second sentence is a result of the first, so “Therefore” correctly signals cause and effect."},
    {id:"w9",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the best transition.\nThe team's first method was unreliable; ___, they switched to a more robust technique.",choices:["consequently","in contrast","for instance","likewise"],ans:0,exp:"The switch happened because of the unreliable method, so “consequently” (meaning “as a result”) is the right link."},
    {id:"w10",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Which option best combines the sentences?\nThe author wrote the novel. It won several awards.",choices:["The author wrote the novel, which won several awards.","The author wrote the novel, and it won several awards, that were notable.","The novel, written by the author, winning several awards.","Having written the novel, several awards were won by it."],ans:0,exp:"A relative clause (“which won several awards”) combines the ideas clearly. The other options are wordy, incomplete, or have a dangling modifier."},
    {id:"w11",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nBy the time we arrived, the play ___ already ___ .",choices:["has … begun","had … begun","have … began","was … begin"],ans:1,exp:"Two past events are described, and the play started first. Use the past perfect “had begun” for the earlier action."},
    {id:"w12",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nThe team celebrated ___ victory.",choices:["their","its","it's","there"],ans:1,exp:"“Team” is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular pronoun “its.” “It's” means “it is,” and “there” refers to a place."},
    {id:"w13",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nShe enjoys hiking, swimming, and ___ .",choices:["to bike","biking","to ride a bike","bikes"],ans:1,exp:"The list should be parallel. “Hiking” and “swimming” are -ing forms, so the third item should also be “biking.”"},
    {id:"w14",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the sentence with the modifier placed correctly.\nWalking through the park, ___ .",choices:["the flowers were beautiful.","the beautiful flowers caught my eye.","I saw many beautiful flowers.","beautiful flowers were seen."],ans:2,exp:"“Walking through the park” describes whoever is walking, so the subject must be a person. Only “I saw many beautiful flowers” has “I” as the subject."},
    {id:"w15",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the best transition.\nThe park has many walking trails. ___, it features a large lake for fishing.",choices:["However","In addition","For example","Consequently"],ans:1,exp:"The second sentence adds another feature, so “In addition” correctly signals that more information is coming."},
    {id:"w16",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Which choice most effectively introduces the paragraph?\n___ A 2019 study found that trees in urban areas can lower street temperatures by several degrees, making sidewalks more comfortable in summer.",choices:["Trees provide shade.","Trees can reduce urban heat.","Some trees are taller than others.","Parks are pleasant places."],ans:1,exp:"The paragraph is about trees lowering temperatures, so “Trees can reduce urban heat” directly sets up the evidence that follows."},
    {id:"w17",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"A student is writing an essay arguing that school gardens benefit students. Which choice most effectively uses the most relevant information from the notes?\n• Gardens provide hands-on science lessons.\n• Students who garden eat more vegetables.\n• Many schools have unused outdoor space.",choices:["Many schools have unused outdoor space, which is a problem.","School gardens give students hands-on science lessons and encourage healthier eating habits.","Students who garden eat more vegetables, and vegetables are healthy.","Some schools have gardens, and some do not."],ans:1,exp:"The goal is to argue that gardens benefit students, so the best choice uses the two benefit-related notes (science lessons and healthier eating) and skips the less relevant note."},
    {id:"w18",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nThe group of students ___ meeting in the library today.",choices:["is","are","were","be"],ans:0,exp:"“Group” is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb “is.” The phrase “of students” does not change the subject."},
    {id:"w19",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?",choices:["We went to the store; and bought milk.","We went to the store, and bought milk.","We went to the store and bought milk.","We went to the store and, bought milk."],ans:2,exp:"“We went to the store and bought milk” has one subject doing two actions, so no comma is needed before “and.”"},
    {id:"w20",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that best completes the sentence.\nMaria ___ to the store before it started raining.",choices:["goes","went","had gone","will go"],ans:2,exp:"Two past events are described, and going to the store happened first. Use the past perfect “had gone” for the earlier action."},
    {id:"w21",diff:"easy",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the best transition.\nThe movie was long. ___, it was very entertaining.",choices:["However","Therefore","For example","Similarly"],ans:0,exp:"The second clause contrasts with the first (long but entertaining), so “However” is the correct contrast word."},
    {id:"w22",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Which sentence is written correctly?",choices:["He don't have any money.","He doesn't have any money.","He doesn't has any money.","He don't has any money."],ans:1,exp:"With a third-person singular subject (“he”), use “does” and keep the main verb in its base form: “He doesn't have any money.”"},
    {id:"r1",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_BEES,q:"The passage suggests that colony collapse disorder is best understood as…",choices:["the result of a single, unidentified cause","a problem with a single primary cause","the product of several interacting stressors","an unsolvable mystery"],ans:2,exp:"The passage repeatedly says the causes combine and interact rather than pointing to one culprit, so “the product of several interacting stressors” is the best summary."},
    {id:"r2",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_BEES,q:"According to the passage, how does the varroa mite harm bees?",choices:["By reducing available flowering plants","By feeding on the bees","By spreading pesticides","By destroying bee habitats"],ans:1,exp:"The passage states directly that the varroa mite “weakens bees by feeding on them.”"},
    {id:"r3",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_BEES,q:"The last sentence mainly serves to…",choices:["emphasize the economic value of honey","argue that protecting bees is impossible","suggest that multiple threats must be addressed together","question the importance of honeybees"],ans:2,exp:"The final sentence concludes that protection “requires addressing multiple threats at once.”"},
    {id:"r4",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_GARDEN,q:"In the passage, the “single ripe tomato” most likely represents…",choices:["a reason to continue making excuses","a sign that the garden is beyond saving","an invitation to begin restoring the garden","a memory of her grandmother's disappointment"],ans:2,exp:"The text says the tomato “felt like an invitation” and immediately after, Elena begins working."},
    {id:"r5",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_GARDEN,q:"Which choice best describes Elena's change over the course of the passage?",choices:["From determined to discouraged","From hesitant to engaged","From indifferent to nostalgic","From angry to forgiving"],ans:1,exp:"Elena starts by making excuses (hesitant) and ends by actively clearing the soil (engaged)."},
    {id:"r6",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_GARDEN,q:"The phrase “less like a memory and more like a beginning” suggests that Elena…",choices:["plans to sell the garden","has begun a process of renewal","misses her childhood","doubts she can succeed"],ans:1,exp:"“More like a beginning” signals a fresh start, so the phrase suggests a process of renewal."},
    {id:"r7",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_ECON,q:"The main purpose of the passage is to…",choices:["argue that people make rational financial decisions","describe how mental shortcuts can influence decisions","prove that experiments are unreliable","advocate for stricter government regulation"],ans:1,exp:"The passage explains behavioral economics and how heuristics like anchoring shape decisions."},
    {id:"r8",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_ECON,q:"The “anchoring effect” is best described as…",choices:["a preference for the lowest available price","a tendency to rely too heavily on an initial piece of information","a policy that nudges consumers","a bias against large numbers"],ans:1,exp:"The passage defines anchoring as an initial number influencing later judgments — relying too heavily on the first piece of information."},
    {id:"r9",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_ECON,q:"The example of the inflated “original” price illustrates…",choices:["how retailers can exploit the anchoring effect","why sale prices are illegal","that consumers are always rational","a flaw in behavioral economics"],ans:0,exp:"Showing a high “original” price anchors shoppers so the sale price looks better."},
    {id:"v2",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_ECON,q:"As used in the passage, the word “arbitrary” most nearly means…",choices:["random or not based on reason","important","official","measurable"],ans:0,exp:"The passage says even an “arbitrary” starting number influences judgments — a number chosen for no particular reason."},
    {id:"r10",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_HISTORY,q:"The author suggests that modeling the Declaration of Sentiments on the Declaration of Independence was significant because it…",choices:["appealed to a shared national tradition","proved that women could write as well as men","was required by federal law","distanced the movement from politics"],ans:0,exp:"Borrowing the founding language showed women's rights were the “fulfillment” of American ideals — an appeal to shared tradition."},
    {id:"r11",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_HISTORY,q:"According to the passage, which right was not secured until 1920?",choices:["The right to own property","The right to vote","The right to an education","The right to assemble"],ans:1,exp:"The passage states the right to vote was secured nationwide by the Nineteenth Amendment in 1920."},
    {id:"r12",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_HISTORY,q:"The phrase “not a departure from American ideals but their fulfillment” most directly supports which idea?",choices:["Women's rights were a rejection of the Constitution","The movement was inconsistent with American values","Equal rights for women were consistent with the nation's founding principles","The convention was a failed effort"],ans:2,exp:"“Fulfillment” of American ideals means women's rights were consistent with — not against — the nation's founding principles."},
    {id:"r13",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_BIRDS,q:"The passage indicates that urban males sing at higher pitches primarily because…",choices:["females in cities prefer higher pitches for aesthetic reasons","higher-pitched songs are easier to hear over low-frequency traffic noise","higher-pitched songs travel farther in open spaces","urban males are larger than forest males"],ans:1,exp:"The passage explains that low-frequency traffic noise masks lower notes, so higher-pitched songs are easier to hear."},
    {id:"r14",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_BIRDS,q:"The researchers' use of playback recordings most directly served to…",choices:["measure how far songs travel","test how females in each environment responded to different songs","train males to sing at higher pitches","eliminate traffic noise from cities"],ans:1,exp:"Playback let the researchers see how urban and forest females responded to each song type."},
    {id:"r15",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_BIRDS,q:"The final sentence implies that human noise may…",choices:["only temporarily affect animal behavior","have lasting effects on animal populations","benefit all urban wildlife","have no measurable impact"],ans:1,exp:"The study suggests noise can “drive evolutionary change,” which is a lasting effect on populations."},
    {id:"v1",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_BIRDS,q:"In the passage, the word “masks” most nearly means…",choices:["covers or hides","decorates","removes","measures"],ans:0,exp:"The passage says traffic noise “masks” the lower notes, meaning it covers or hides them so they're hard to hear."},
    {id:"r16",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_LETTER,q:"The comparison of the man's sentences to “startled birds” emphasizes…",choices:["the beauty of his planned speech","how quickly and completely his prepared words left him","his anger at being interrupted","his skill as an orator"],ans:1,exp:"Startled birds fly away suddenly. The simile shows his prepared words vanished quickly and completely."},
    {id:"r17",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_LETTER,q:"According to the passage, the man had not opened the letter because he…",choices:["could not find it","feared its contents","preferred the unresolved question over an answer","had forgotten about it"],ans:2,exp:"The passage says he “preferred” the open question “over any answer.”"},
    {id:"r18",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_LETTER,q:"The final image of the letter “patient as stone” most strongly suggests…",choices:["the letter is valuable","the man's question will endure","the man has finally forgotten the letter","the letter is physically heavy"],ans:1,exp:"Stone is steady and unchanging, so the simile implies the unanswered question remains, waiting."},
    {id:"r19",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_SLEEP,q:"Which choice best states the main purpose of the passage?",choices:["to prove that sleep is dangerous","to present a leading theory for why sleep is necessary","to argue that animals should sleep less","to describe how memories form during sleep"],ans:1,exp:"The passage introduces the waste-clearance theory as an influential explanation for why sleep is necessary."},
    {id:"r20",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_SLEEP,q:"According to the passage, the finding that the space between brain cells expands during sleep supports the idea that sleep…",choices:["consolidates memories","helps flush waste from the brain","regulates hormones","repairs damaged cells"],ans:1,exp:"The expanded space increases fluid flow that “flushes out waste,” supporting the waste-clearance theory."},
    {id:"r21",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_TRADE,q:"According to the passage, a country has a comparative advantage when it can produce a good…",choices:["using fewer resources than another country","at a lower opportunity cost than another country","more quickly than another country","without any opportunity cost"],ans:1,exp:"The passage defines comparative advantage as producing at a “lower opportunity cost.”"},
    {id:"r22",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:PASS_TRADE,q:"The author most likely mentions David Ricardo to…",choices:["show that trade theory is outdated","identify the economist who developed the idea of comparative advantage","argue against international trade","explain how opportunity cost is calculated"],ans:1,exp:"Ricardo is named as the person who “developed” the idea, so the mention credits the idea to him."}
  ]
};

const ACT_SCIENCE = [
  {id:"s1",diff:"easy",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A student measured the boiling point of water at different altitudes.\nAltitude (m): 0 · 500 · 1000 · 2000\nBoiling point (°C): 100 · 98 · 96 · 93\nBased on the data, what happens to the boiling point as altitude increases?",choices:["It increases","It stays the same","It decreases","It first increases, then decreases"],ans:2,exp:"At 0 m it's 100°C and at 2000 m it's 93°C — the boiling point steadily falls as altitude rises."},
  {id:"s2",diff:"medium",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A student measured the boiling point of water at different altitudes.\nAltitude (m): 0 · 500 · 1000 · 2000\nBoiling point (°C): 100 · 98 · 96 · 93\nAt an altitude of 1500 m, the boiling point is most likely…",choices:["99°C","97°C","95°C","91°C"],ans:2,exp:"1500 m is halfway between 1000 m (96°C) and 2000 m (93°C), so about 94–95°C. 95°C is the closest estimate."},
  {id:"s3",diff:"medium",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"A student rolled a ball down ramps of different angles and measured the time to reach the bottom.\nAngle: 10° · 20° · 30° · 40°\nTime (s): 4.0 · 2.8 · 2.2 · 1.8\nWhich statement best describes the relationship?",choices:["As angle increases, time increases","As angle increases, time decreases","Angle has no effect on time","Time first increases, then decreases"],ans:1,exp:"Time drops from 4.0 s to 1.8 s as the angle rises, so steeper ramps make the ball roll faster."},
  {id:"s4",diff:"medium",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"A student rolled a ball down ramps of different angles.\nAngle: 10° · 20° · 30° · 40°\nTime (s): 4.0 · 2.8 · 2.2 · 1.8\nIf the student tested a 50° ramp, the roll time would most likely be…",choices:["greater than 4.0 s","between 2.8 and 4.0 s","between 1.8 and 2.2 s","less than 1.8 s"],ans:3,exp:"The pattern shows time keeps decreasing as angle increases, so a 50° ramp should be faster than 1.8 s."},
  {id:"s5",diff:"easy",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A table shows a plant's height each week.\nWeek: 1 · 2 · 3 · 4\nHeight (cm): 5 · 8 · 11 · 14\nHow much did the plant grow each week?",choices:["2 cm","3 cm","4 cm","5 cm"],ans:1,exp:"The height increases by 3 cm each week (5 → 8 → 11 → 14)."},
  {id:"s6",diff:"medium",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A table shows a plant's height each week.\nWeek: 1 · 2 · 3 · 4\nHeight (cm): 5 · 8 · 11 · 14\nIf the pattern continues, what is the most likely height at week 6?",choices:["17 cm","20 cm","23 cm","26 cm"],ans:1,exp:"Adding 3 cm per week: week 5 = 17 cm, week 6 = 20 cm."},
  {id:"s7",diff:"hard",domain:"Conflicting Viewpoints",q:"Two scientists explain why the dinosaurs went extinct.\nScientist 1: Massive volcanic eruptions released gases that cooled the planet.\nScientist 2: An asteroid impact threw dust into the air that blocked sunlight.\nWhich observation would most directly support Scientist 2?",choices:["Lava flows dated to the extinction","A large crater and an iridium-rich layer dated to the extinction","Gradual climate change over millions of years","Fossils showing dinosaurs were already declining"],ans:1,exp:"A crater and an iridium layer are classic signatures of an asteroid impact, so they support Scientist 2."},
  {id:"s8",diff:"hard",domain:"Conflicting Viewpoints",q:"Two scientists explain why the dinosaurs went extinct.\nScientist 1: Massive volcanic eruptions released gases that cooled the planet.\nScientist 2: An asteroid impact threw dust into the air that blocked sunlight.\nScientist 1 and Scientist 2 would most likely agree on which point?",choices:["Dinosaurs went extinct relatively suddenly","The extinction was caused by volcanoes only","An asteroid hit Earth","Climate change played no role"],ans:0,exp:"Both describe a sudden catastrophic event, so they'd agree the extinction was relatively sudden."},
  {id:"s9",diff:"medium",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"In an experiment, plants given more fertilizer grew taller — but only up to a point. Above a certain amount, plants actually grew shorter.\nWhat does this suggest about fertilizer?",choices:["More fertilizer is always better","Fertilizer has no effect on growth","There is an optimal amount of fertilizer","Fertilizer only affects leaves"],ans:2,exp:"Growth increases to a peak then declines, which means there's a “best” amount — not too little, not too much."},
  {id:"s10",diff:"medium",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A table shows the freezing point of salt water at different salt concentrations.\nSalt (%): 0 · 5 · 10 · 20\nFreezing point (°C): 0 · −3 · −6 · −12\nAs the salt concentration increases, the freezing point…",choices:["increases","stays the same","decreases","first increases, then decreases"],ans:2,exp:"The freezing point drops from 0°C to −12°C as salt increases, so it decreases."},
  {id:"s11",diff:"hard",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A table shows the freezing point of salt water at different salt concentrations.\nSalt (%): 0 · 5 · 10 · 20\nFreezing point (°C): 0 · −3 · −6 · −12\nBased on the trend, the freezing point at 25% salt is most likely…",choices:["−9°C","−12°C","−15°C","−18°C"],ans:2,exp:"From 10% to 20% the freezing point drops 6°C (−6 to −12). Continuing the trend, 25% should be around −15°C."},
  {id:"s12",diff:"easy",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"A pendulum of different lengths was timed.\nLength (cm): 50 · 100 · 200\nSwings in 10 s: 10 · 7 · 5\nWhat can be concluded?",choices:["Longer pendulums swing more times in 10 s","Longer pendulums swing fewer times in 10 s","Length does not affect swing rate","Shorter pendulums swing fewer times"],ans:1,exp:"As length increases, swings drop from 10 to 5, so longer pendulums swing fewer times (more slowly)."}
];
// ---- EXPANDED BANK: original questions written in the style and format of the digital SAT and ACT ----
BANK.math.push(
  {id:"m101",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"The equation 4x − 9 = 3x + 2 is true for what value of x?",choices:["−11","−7","7","11"],ans:3,exp:"Subtract 3x from both sides: x − 9 = 2. Add 9: x = 11."},
  {id:"m102",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"A gym charges a $30 sign-up fee and $15 per month. Which expression gives the total cost, in dollars, for m months?",choices:["15m + 30","30m + 15","45m","15(m + 30)"],ans:0,exp:"The $30 is paid once and $15 is paid each month, so the cost is 15m + 30."},
  {id:"m103",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A store sold 48 shirts on Saturday, which was 3 times the number sold on Monday. How many shirts were sold on Monday?",choices:["12","16","45","144"],ans:1,exp:"If 3 × Monday = 48, then Monday = 48 ÷ 3 = 16."},
  {id:"m104",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"Line ℓ is defined by y = −2x + 7. What is the y-intercept of line ℓ?",choices:["(0, −2)","(0, 7)","(7, 0)","(−2, 0)"],ans:1,exp:"In y = mx + b, b is the y-intercept. Here b = 7, so the line crosses the y-axis at (0, 7)."},
  {id:"m105",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"There are 12 inches in 1 foot. How many inches are in 4.5 feet?",choices:["16.5","48","54","60"],ans:2,exp:"Multiply 4.5 × 12 = 54 inches."},
  {id:"m106",diff:"easy",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Which expression is equivalent to 5(x + 2) − 3x?",choices:["2x + 2","2x + 10","8x + 10","2x + 7"],ans:1,exp:"Distribute: 5x + 10 − 3x = 2x + 10."},
  {id:"m107",diff:"easy",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A square has a perimeter of 36 centimeters. What is the area of the square, in square centimeters?",choices:["9","36","81","144"],ans:2,exp:"Each side is 36 ÷ 4 = 9 cm, so the area is 9² = 81 square centimeters."},
  {id:"m108",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A data set consists of the values 6, 6, 8, 10, and 15. What is the mode of the data set?",choices:["6","8","9","15"],ans:0,exp:"The mode is the value that appears most often. 6 appears twice."},
  {id:"m109",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"If 2(n + 3) = 18, what is the value of n + 3?",choices:["6","9","12","15"],ans:1,exp:"Divide both sides by 2: n + 3 = 9. The SAT often asks for an expression — you don't need n itself."},
  {id:"m110",diff:"easy",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"If h(x) = x² + 1, what is h(−3)?",choices:["−8","7","10","−5"],ans:2,exp:"(−3)² = 9, and 9 + 1 = 10. Squaring a negative gives a positive."},
  {id:"m111",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A survey of 200 students found that 35% prefer online classes. How many students prefer online classes?",choices:["35","65","70","130"],ans:2,exp:"35% of 200 = 0.35 × 200 = 70."},
  {id:"m112",diff:"easy",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"Two lines intersect, forming a pair of vertical angles. One angle measures 47°. What is the measure of the angle vertical to it?",choices:["43°","47°","133°","313°"],ans:1,exp:"Vertical angles are congruent, so it also measures 47°."},
  {id:"m113",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"Which ordered pair (x, y) is a solution to the inequality y > 2x − 1?",choices:["(1, 0)","(2, 3)","(0, 1)","(3, 4)"],ans:2,exp:"Test (0, 1): 1 > 2(0) − 1 = −1. True. The others give 0 > 1, 3 > 3, and 4 > 5, which are false."},
  {id:"m114",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A printer prints 18 pages per minute. At this rate, how many minutes will it take to print 270 pages?",choices:["12","15","18","25"],ans:1,exp:"270 ÷ 18 = 15 minutes."},
  {id:"m115",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"The function f is defined by f(x) = 7x − 5. What is the value of f(x + 1)?",choices:["7x − 4","7x + 2","7x + 1","7x − 5"],ans:1,exp:"Replace x with (x + 1): 7(x + 1) − 5 = 7x + 7 − 5 = 7x + 2."},
  {id:"m116",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"3x + 2y = 16\nx − 2y = 0\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system of equations?",choices:["(2, 5)","(4, 2)","(8, 4)","(6, −1)"],ans:1,exp:"Add the equations: 4x = 16, so x = 4. Then 4 − 2y = 0 gives y = 2."},
  {id:"m117",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"A line in the xy-plane passes through (−1, 4) and (3, −4). What is the slope of the line?",choices:["−2","−1/2","1/2","2"],ans:0,exp:"Slope = (−4 − 4)/(3 − (−1)) = −8/4 = −2."},
  {id:"m118",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Which of the following is a factor of x² + 2x − 15?",choices:["x − 5","x + 3","x − 3","x + 15"],ans:2,exp:"Find numbers that multiply to −15 and add to 2: 5 and −3. So x² + 2x − 15 = (x + 5)(x − 3)."},
  {id:"m119",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A jacket's price was reduced by 30% to $56. What was the original price?",choices:["$72.80","$78.00","$80.00","$86.00"],ans:2,exp:"After a 30% cut, the price is 70% of the original: 0.70p = 56, so p = 80."},
  {id:"m120",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"The equation y = 12x + 150 models the total amount y, in dollars, in a savings account after x weeks. What is the best interpretation of 12 in this context?",choices:["The starting balance","The amount added each week","The total after 12 weeks","The number of weeks"],ans:1,exp:"12 is the slope — the change in dollars per week. 150 is the starting balance."},
  {id:"m121",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"What is the sum of the solutions to (x − 4)(x + 9) = 0?",choices:["−13","−5","5","13"],ans:1,exp:"The solutions are x = 4 and x = −9. Their sum is 4 + (−9) = −5."},
  {id:"m122",diff:"medium",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF, with AB corresponding to DE. If AB = 6, DE = 15, and BC = 8, what is EF?",choices:["17","18","20","24"],ans:2,exp:"The scale factor is 15/6 = 2.5. EF = 8 × 2.5 = 20."},
  {id:"m123",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"In a class, the ratio of juniors to seniors is 5 to 3. If there are 40 students in the class, how many are seniors?",choices:["12","15","24","25"],ans:1,exp:"The ratio has 5 + 3 = 8 parts, so each part is 40 ÷ 8 = 5 students. Seniors = 3 × 5 = 15."},
  {id:"m124",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Which expression is equivalent to (x²y³)(x⁴y)?",choices:["x⁶y³","x⁸y³","x⁶y⁴","x⁸y⁴"],ans:2,exp:"Add exponents of like bases: x²⁺⁴ = x⁶ and y³⁺¹ = y⁴."},
  {id:"m125",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"For what value of k does the system have no solution?\ny = kx + 3\ny = 4x − 1",choices:["−4","1/4","3","4"],ans:3,exp:"Two lines with the same slope and different intercepts are parallel and never meet. So k = 4."},
  {id:"m126",diff:"medium",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A right circular cone has radius 3 and height 8. What is its volume? (V = ⅓πr²h)",choices:["8π","24π","72π","96π"],ans:1,exp:"V = ⅓π(3²)(8) = ⅓π(72) = 24π."},
  {id:"m127",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A random sample of 400 voters in a city found that 52% support a new park, with a margin of error of 4%. Which conclusion is most appropriate?",choices:["Exactly 52% of the city's voters support the park.","The true percentage is plausibly between 48% and 56%.","Most of the city's voters definitely support the park.","The survey is invalid because 400 is too small."],ans:1,exp:"A margin of error gives a plausible range: 52% ± 4% = 48% to 56%. Since that range includes values below 50%, we can't say a majority definitely supports it."},
  {id:"m128",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"If 3^(x+1) = 81, what is the value of x?",choices:["2","3","4","26"],ans:1,exp:"81 = 3⁴, so x + 1 = 4 and x = 3."},
  {id:"m129",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"Maria has $500 and spends $35 per week. After how many weeks will she have $255 left?",choices:["5","6","7","8"],ans:2,exp:"500 − 35w = 255 gives 35w = 245, so w = 7."},
  {id:"m130",diff:"medium",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"In a right triangle, cos A = 5/13. What is sin A?",choices:["5/12","12/13","13/12","12/5"],ans:1,exp:"With adjacent 5 and hypotenuse 13, the opposite side is √(169 − 25) = 12. So sin A = 12/13."},
  {id:"m131",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A car's fuel efficiency is 32 miles per gallon. Gas costs $3.50 per gallon. What is the cost of gas, in dollars, to drive 224 miles?",choices:["$21.00","$24.50","$28.00","$31.50"],ans:1,exp:"224 ÷ 32 = 7 gallons; 7 × $3.50 = $24.50."},
  {id:"m132",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"The function g is defined by g(x) = (x − 2)² − 9. What is the minimum value of g?",choices:["−9","−5","2","9"],ans:0,exp:"In vertex form a(x − h)² + k with a > 0, the minimum value is k = −9 (at x = 2)."},
  {id:"m133",diff:"medium",domain:"Algebra",q:"Which equation represents a line perpendicular to y = (2/3)x + 1?",choices:["y = (2/3)x − 4","y = −(2/3)x + 1","y = −(3/2)x + 5","y = (3/2)x"],ans:2,exp:"Perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals. The negative reciprocal of 2/3 is −3/2."},
  {id:"m134",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"The table shows the number of pets owned by 20 students.\nPets: 0 · 1 · 2 · 3\nStudents: 4 · 9 · 5 · 2\nWhat is the median number of pets?",choices:["0","1","1.5","2"],ans:1,exp:"With 20 values, the median is the average of the 10th and 11th. Values 1–4 are 0 and values 5–13 are 1, so both are 1."},
  {id:"m135",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"For the quadratic y = x² − 8x + c, the graph touches the x-axis at exactly one point. What is the value of c?",choices:["4","8","16","64"],ans:2,exp:"One x-intercept means the discriminant is 0: (−8)² − 4(1)(c) = 0 → 64 = 4c → c = 16."},
  {id:"m136",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"A population of bacteria doubles every 3 hours. If there are 500 bacteria now, which function gives the number after t hours?",choices:["P(t) = 500(2)^(3t)","P(t) = 500(2)^(t/3)","P(t) = 500(3)^(t/2)","P(t) = 1000t/3"],ans:1,exp:"The population doubles once per 3 hours, so the number of doublings after t hours is t/3: P = 500·2^(t/3)."},
  {id:"m137",diff:"hard",domain:"Algebra",q:"If 2x + 3y = 12 and 4x + 6y = k has infinitely many solutions, what is k?",choices:["6","12","18","24"],ans:3,exp:"Infinitely many solutions means the equations are the same line. The second is the first times 2, so k = 24."},
  {id:"m138",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Which expression is equivalent to (x² − 16)/(x + 4), for x ≠ −4?",choices:["x − 4","x + 4","x − 16","x² − 4"],ans:0,exp:"Factor: (x − 4)(x + 4)/(x + 4) = x − 4."},
  {id:"m139",diff:"hard",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A circle in the xy-plane has equation x² + y² − 6x + 4y = 12. What is the radius of the circle?",choices:["2√3","5","12","25"],ans:1,exp:"Complete the square: (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 12 + 9 + 4 = 25. The radius is √25 = 5."},
  {id:"m140",diff:"hard",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"The mean score of 10 students was 80. When one more student's score was added, the mean became 82. What was the new student's score?",choices:["82","92","100","102"],ans:3,exp:"Original total = 800. New total = 11 × 82 = 902. The new score = 902 − 800 = 102."},
  {id:"m141",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"If x > 0 and x² − 5x − 14 = 0, what is the value of x?",choices:["2","5","7","14"],ans:2,exp:"Factor: (x − 7)(x + 2) = 0. Since x > 0, x = 7."},
  {id:"m142",diff:"hard",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"In a circle with radius 10, a central angle measures 72°. What is the length of the arc it intercepts?",choices:["2π","4π","10π","20π"],ans:1,exp:"72° is 72/360 = 1/5 of the circle. Circumference = 20π, so the arc = 20π/5 = 4π."},
  {id:"m143",diff:"hard",domain:"Algebra",q:"A phone plan costs $40 per month plus $0.10 per text over 500 texts. If a bill was $52.50, how many texts were sent?",choices:["125","525","625","1,025"],ans:2,exp:"Overage = 52.50 − 40 = 12.50, which is 125 texts at $0.10. Total texts = 500 + 125 = 625."},
  {id:"m144",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"The function f(x) = 2(x + 1)(x − 5) is graphed in the xy-plane. What is the x-coordinate of the vertex?",choices:["−1","2","3","5"],ans:1,exp:"The vertex lies midway between the zeros −1 and 5: (−1 + 5)/2 = 2."},
  {id:"m145",diff:"hard",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A value increased by 20% and then decreased by 20%. The final value is what percent of the original?",choices:["96%","100%","104%","80%"],ans:0,exp:"Multiply the factors: 1.20 × 0.80 = 0.96, so the final value is 96% of the original."},
  {id:"m146",diff:"hard",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"In right triangle XYZ, angle Y is 90°. If sin X = 0.6, what is cos Z?",choices:["0.4","0.6","0.8","1.25"],ans:1,exp:"X and Z are complementary, and the sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement. So cos Z = sin X = 0.6."},
  {id:"m147",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"If (ax + 3)(2x − 1) = 6x² + bx − 3 for all x, what is the value of b?",choices:["−3","3","5","9"],ans:1,exp:"The x² term: 2a = 6, so a = 3. The x term: −a + 6 = −3 + 6 = 3. So b = 3."},
  {id:"m148",diff:"hard",domain:"Algebra",q:"The graph of y = |x − 2| − 3 has its vertex at which point?",choices:["(−2, −3)","(2, 3)","(2, −3)","(−2, 3)"],ans:2,exp:"y = |x − h| + k has vertex (h, k). Here h = 2 and k = −3."},
  {id:"m149",diff:"hard",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"Data set A: 10, 20, 30, 40, 50. Data set B: 28, 29, 30, 31, 32. Which statement is true?",choices:["A and B have the same mean, and A has a greater standard deviation.","A and B have the same mean, and B has a greater standard deviation.","A has a greater mean.","They have the same standard deviation."],ans:0,exp:"Both have mean 30. A's values are much more spread out, so A has the larger standard deviation."},
  {id:"m150",diff:"hard",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"For what positive value of x is √(x + 6) = x?",choices:["2","3","6","9"],ans:1,exp:"Square: x + 6 = x² → x² − x − 6 = 0 → (x − 3)(x + 2) = 0. Only x = 3 works since √ is nonnegative."},
  {id:"m151",diff:"medium",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A rectangular box has length 5, width 4, and height 3. What is its volume?",choices:["12","47","60","94"],ans:2,exp:"V = l × w × h = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60."},
  {id:"m152",diff:"easy",domain:"Algebra",q:"If 6y = 42, what is the value of 3y?",choices:["7","14","21","126"],ans:2,exp:"3y is half of 6y, so 3y = 42 ÷ 2 = 21."},
  {id:"m153",diff:"medium",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A bakery makes 3 kinds of muffins and 4 kinds of drinks. How many different muffin-and-drink combos are possible?",choices:["7","12","24","64"],ans:1,exp:"Multiply the choices: 3 × 4 = 12."},
  {id:"m154",diff:"easy",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"A map uses a scale where 1 inch represents 25 miles. Two towns are 3.2 inches apart on the map. How far apart are they in miles?",choices:["28.2","75","80","85"],ans:2,exp:"3.2 × 25 = 80 miles."},
  {id:"m155",diff:"hard",domain:"Algebra",q:"A boat travels 36 miles downstream in 2 hours and returns upstream in 3 hours. What is the speed of the current, in miles per hour?",choices:["3","6","12","15"],ans:0,exp:"Downstream speed 18 = b + c; upstream 12 = b − c. Subtracting: 2c = 6, so c = 3."},
  {id:"m156",diff:"medium",domain:"Advanced Math",q:"Which of the following is equivalent to x^(3/2)?",choices:["√(x³)","∛(x²)","3x/2","x³/2"],ans:0,exp:"x^(m/n) = ⁿ√(x^m). So x^(3/2) = √(x³)."},
  {id:"m157",diff:"easy",domain:"Geometry & Trigonometry",q:"A triangle has angles of 90° and 35°. What is the third angle?",choices:["35°","45°","55°","65°"],ans:2,exp:"180 − 90 − 35 = 55°."},
  {id:"m158",diff:"hard",domain:"Problem-Solving & Data Analysis",q:"In a group of 60 people, 25 own a cat, 30 own a dog, and 10 own both. How many own neither?",choices:["5","10","15","25"],ans:2,exp:"Owners of at least one = 25 + 30 − 10 = 45. Neither = 60 − 45 = 15."}
);

BANK.rw.push(
  {id:"w101",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe flock of geese ___ south every October, stopping at the same lake along the way.",choices:["fly","flies","are flying","have flown"],ans:1,exp:"The subject is “flock” (singular), not “geese,” so the verb must be singular: “flies.”"},
  {id:"w102",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nMarine biologist Sylvia Earle has spent more than 7,000 hours ___ studying ocean ecosystems firsthand.",choices:["underwater,","underwater;","underwater","underwater:"],ans:2,exp:"No punctuation is needed between “underwater” and the phrase that follows; any mark would interrupt the sentence."},
  {id:"w103",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe museum's newest exhibit features ___ from three different centuries.",choices:["artifact's","artifacts","artifacts'","artifact"],ans:1,exp:"A simple plural is needed, with no possession: “artifacts.”"},
  {id:"w104",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe novel was well received by ___ and became a bestseller within a month.",choices:["critics,","critics","critics;","critics:"],ans:1,exp:"“and became” shares the subject “The novel,” so no comma is needed before “and.”"},
  {id:"w105",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe Atacama Desert is one of the driest places on ___ some weather stations there have never recorded rain.",choices:["Earth,","Earth;","Earth","Earth, and,"],ans:1,exp:"Both sides are complete sentences. A semicolon correctly joins two independent clauses; a comma alone would create a comma splice."},
  {id:"w106",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nAfter analyzing the soil samples, ___",choices:["the results surprised the researchers.","the researchers were surprised by the results.","surprise was felt by the researchers.","the samples showed surprising results."],ans:1,exp:"The opening phrase must describe whoever did the analyzing — the researchers. Otherwise it's a dangling modifier."},
  {id:"w107",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe architect Zaha Hadid, known for her sweeping curved ___ was the first woman to win the Pritzker Prize.",choices:["designs","designs,","designs;","designs—"],ans:1,exp:"The phrase “known for her sweeping curved designs” is set off by commas. The opening comma needs a matching closing comma."},
  {id:"w108",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nEach of the paintings in the gallery ___ been restored at least once.",choices:["have","has","are","were"],ans:1,exp:"“Each” is the singular subject, so it takes “has.” The phrase “of the paintings” doesn't change that."},
  {id:"w109",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe expedition needed three ___ a satellite phone, a water purifier, and a solar charger.",choices:["things,","things:","things;","things"],ans:1,exp:"A colon introduces a list after a complete sentence."},
  {id:"w110",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe team's findings, which were published last year, ___ that coral can recover faster than expected.",choices:["suggests","suggest","suggesting","has suggested"],ans:1,exp:"The subject is “findings” (plural), so the verb is “suggest.”"},
  {id:"w111",diff:"hard",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nIn 1969, the Apollo 11 lunar module ___ on the Moon's surface, and millions of viewers watched the broadcast.",choices:["lands","landed","has landed","had been landing"],ans:1,exp:"The event happened at a specific past time (1969), and the sentence pairs it with “watched,” so the simple past “landed” fits."},
  {id:"w112",diff:"hard",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe poet's later work is more ___ her early poems were often rhymed, her later ones abandon formal structure almost entirely.",choices:["experimental, while","experimental: while","experimental; while","experimental, while,"],ans:1,exp:"A colon introduces an explanation of the first clause. The second part explains how the work is more experimental."},
  {id:"w113",diff:"hard",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe researchers compared the two ___ the first grown in shade, the second in full sun.",choices:["plants:","plants;","plants","plants, and"],ans:0,exp:"After a complete clause, a colon introduces the elaboration. A semicolon would need a complete clause after it, which “the first grown in shade…” is not."},
  {id:"w114",diff:"easy",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\nThe city expected the new bridge to reduce traffic. ___ congestion actually increased in the first year, as more drivers chose the new route.",choices:["Similarly,","However,","Therefore,","For example,"],ans:1,exp:"The result (more congestion) contrasts with the expectation, so “However” is the logical transition."},
  {id:"w115",diff:"easy",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\nOctopuses can change both the color and the texture of their skin. ___ some species can make their skin look like rough rock or flowing seaweed.",choices:["In contrast,","For instance,","Nevertheless,","Meanwhile,"],ans:1,exp:"The second sentence gives a specific example of the first claim, so “For instance” fits."},
  {id:"w116",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\nEarly telescopes produced blurry images because of flaws in their lenses. ___ astronomers began using curved mirrors, which avoided many of these distortions.",choices:["As a result,","For example,","Likewise,","Even so,"],ans:0,exp:"Using mirrors was a response to the lens problem, so a cause-and-effect transition — “As a result” — fits."},
  {id:"w117",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\nMost songbirds learn their songs by imitating adults. The brown-headed cowbird, ___ is raised by other species and still sings its own species' song.",choices:["consequently,","similarly,","by contrast,","in other words,"],ans:2,exp:"The cowbird is an exception to the rule just stated, so “by contrast” fits."},
  {id:"w118",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries.\n• Most of the existing wall dates from the Ming dynasty (1368–1644).\n• The Ming wall stretches about 5,500 miles.\nThe student wants to emphasize the length of the Ming-era wall. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?",choices:["The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries.","Built during the Ming dynasty, the existing wall stretches about 5,500 miles.","The Ming dynasty lasted from 1368 to 1644.","The Great Wall was built by several dynasties."],ans:1,exp:"Only this choice states the length (5,500 miles), which is what the student wants to emphasize."},
  {id:"w119",diff:"medium",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Mae Jemison was the first Black woman to travel to space.\n• She flew aboard the space shuttle Endeavour in 1992.\n• Before NASA, she worked as a doctor in the Peace Corps.\nThe student wants to introduce Jemison to an audience unfamiliar with her. Which choice most effectively accomplishes this goal?",choices:["In 1992, the space shuttle Endeavour launched.","Mae Jemison, a former Peace Corps doctor, became the first Black woman in space when she flew aboard Endeavour in 1992.","Jemison worked in the Peace Corps.","Many astronauts have flown aboard Endeavour."],ans:1,exp:"An introduction should say who she is and why she matters. This choice does both."},
  {id:"w120",diff:"hard",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Tardigrades are microscopic animals.\n• They can survive temperatures from −272°C to 150°C.\n• Bdelloid rotifers, another microscopic animal, can survive being frozen for thousands of years.\nThe student wants to highlight a similarity between the two animals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?",choices:["Tardigrades can survive temperatures as high as 150°C.","Both tardigrades and bdelloid rotifers are microscopic animals that can survive extreme cold.","Bdelloid rotifers have been frozen for thousands of years.","Tardigrades are more resilient than bdelloid rotifers."],ans:1,exp:"Only this choice compares the two animals and names something they share."},
  {id:"w121",diff:"hard",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\nThe theory predicted that the particle would decay within a microsecond. The experiment, ___ detected particles lasting nearly ten times longer, forcing physicists to reconsider the model.",choices:["accordingly,","however,","for example,","in addition,"],ans:1,exp:"The experimental result contradicts the prediction, so “however” is the logical transition."},
  {id:"w122",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe students handed in ___ projects before the deadline.",choices:["there","their","they're","there's"],ans:1,exp:"“Their” is the possessive form — the projects belong to the students."},
  {id:"w123",diff:"easy",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nNeither the manager nor the employees ___ aware of the change.",choices:["was","were","is","has been"],ans:1,exp:"With “neither…nor,” the verb agrees with the closer subject, “employees,” which is plural."},
  {id:"w124",diff:"medium",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe company released ___ annual report on Monday.",choices:["it's","its","its'","their"],ans:1,exp:"“Its” is the possessive of a singular noun (company). “It's” means “it is.”"},
  {id:"w125",diff:"hard",domain:"Standard English Conventions",kind:"writing",q:"Choose the option that conforms to the conventions of Standard English.\nThe migration of monarch butterflies, which can span ___ is one of the longest insect migrations in the world.",choices:["3,000 miles","3,000 miles,","3,000 miles;","3,000 miles—"],ans:1,exp:"The nonessential clause “which can span 3,000 miles” must be closed with a comma to match the one that opened it."},
  {id:"w126",diff:"easy",domain:"Expression of Ideas",kind:"writing",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\nSolar panels have become much cheaper over the past decade. ___ more households are installing them than ever before.",choices:["Nonetheless,","Consequently,","In contrast,","Previously,"],ans:1,exp:"Cheaper panels lead to more installations — a cause-and-effect relationship, so “Consequently.”"}
);

BANK.rw.push(
  {id:"r101",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"The following text is adapted from Jane Austen's 1813 novel Pride and Prejudice.\nMr. Bennet was so odd a mixture of quick parts, sarcastic humour, reserve, and caprice, that the experience of three-and-twenty years had been insufficient to make his wife understand his character.",q:"As used in the text, what does the word “parts” most nearly mean?",choices:["Pieces","Abilities","Roles","Regions"],ans:1,exp:"“Quick parts” describes Mr. Bennet's mind — a quick intelligence or set of abilities."},
  {id:"r102",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"Many people assume that the ancient Maya abandoned their cities suddenly. Archaeologist Arlen Chase argues that the decline was ___ : population fell gradually over more than a century as droughts made farming less reliable.",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",choices:["abrupt","protracted","celebrated","imaginary"],ans:1,exp:"The text says the decline happened “gradually over more than a century,” so “protracted” (drawn out) fits."},
  {id:"r103",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"Historian Kerri Greenidge's biography of the Grimké family is neither a celebration nor a condemnation. Instead, Greenidge takes a ___ approach, carefully documenting both the family's activism and the contradictions in their lives.",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",choices:["partisan","nuanced","dismissive","whimsical"],ans:1,exp:"A treatment that shows both strengths and contradictions, without simply praising or condemning, is “nuanced.”"},
  {id:"r104",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"Researchers had long believed that plants could not respond to sound. A 2019 study challenged this view: when evening primrose flowers were exposed to the sound of a flying bee, they produced sweeter nectar within three minutes.",q:"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?",choices:["It presents evidence that calls a long-held belief into question.","It explains why researchers held the earlier belief.","It offers an alternative explanation for how plants make nectar.","It summarizes the history of plant research."],ans:0,exp:"The second sentence describes a study whose result “challenged this view.”"},
  {id:"r105",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"Text 1\nSome economists argue that raising the minimum wage reduces employment, since businesses facing higher costs will hire fewer workers.\nText 2\nA study by David Card and Alan Krueger compared fast-food restaurants in New Jersey, which raised its minimum wage, with those in neighboring Pennsylvania, which did not. They found no decrease in employment in New Jersey.",q:"Based on the texts, how would Card and Krueger (Text 2) most likely respond to the economists in Text 1?",choices:["By agreeing that higher wages always reduce employment","By pointing out that their evidence did not show the predicted decrease in employment","By arguing that Pennsylvania should lower its minimum wage","By claiming that businesses never respond to higher costs"],ans:1,exp:"Card and Krueger's data found no decrease in employment, which directly challenges the prediction in Text 1."},
  {id:"r106",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"Honeyguides are African birds that lead people to bees' nests. Researchers found that when honey hunters in Mozambique made a specific trilling call, the birds were more than twice as likely to guide them to a nest as when other sounds were used.",q:"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?",choices:["Honeyguides eat only honey.","Honeyguides respond to a particular human call by guiding people to bees' nests.","Honey hunters in Mozambique no longer use calls.","Researchers trained honeyguides to make a trilling sound."],ans:1,exp:"The key finding is that the specific call made the birds much more likely to guide hunters."},
  {id:"r107",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"Ecologist Hiro Tanaka hypothesized that a particular wildflower depends on bumblebees for pollination. To test this, he covered some flowers with fine mesh that kept out bumblebees but allowed smaller insects through.",q:"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Tanaka's hypothesis?",choices:["Covered and uncovered flowers produced similar numbers of seeds.","Covered flowers produced far fewer seeds than uncovered flowers.","Smaller insects visited the covered flowers often.","Bumblebees visited many other types of flowers nearby."],ans:1,exp:"If excluding bumblebees sharply reduces seed production, that supports the idea that the flower depends on them."},
  {id:"r108",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"A researcher studying a city's bike-share program recorded the average daily number of rides in each season.\nWinter: 1,200 · Spring: 3,400 · Summer: 4,100 · Fall: 2,800\nThe researcher claims that ridership is closely tied to warm weather.",q:"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the researcher's claim?",choices:["Ridership was 2,800 rides per day in fall.","Ridership was highest in summer (4,100) and lowest in winter (1,200).","Spring had more rides than fall.","Ridership varied across seasons."],ans:1,exp:"Contrasting the warmest season (highest) with the coldest (lowest) directly supports a link to warm weather."},
  {id:"r109",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"In 1977, NASA launched the Voyager 1 probe to study Jupiter and Saturn. After completing that mission, the probe kept traveling outward, and in 2012 it became the first human-made object to enter interstellar space.",q:"According to the text, what happened after Voyager 1 completed its study of Jupiter and Saturn?",choices:["It returned to Earth.","It continued traveling and eventually entered interstellar space.","It was redirected to study Mars.","It stopped sending data."],ans:1,exp:"The text says the probe “kept traveling outward” and entered interstellar space in 2012."},
  {id:"r110",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"Some scholars have assumed that medieval European farmers rarely traveled far from home. However, isotope analysis of skeletons from a 12th-century English village showed that nearly a third of the people buried there had grown up more than 100 miles away. This suggests that ___",q:"Which choice most logically completes the text?",choices:["medieval farmers never left their villages.","migration in medieval England may have been more common than scholars have assumed.","isotope analysis is unreliable.","the village was founded in the 12th century."],ans:1,exp:"Many people from far away challenges the assumption that farmers rarely traveled, so migration was likely more common than assumed."},
  {id:"r111",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"The following text is from Zora Neale Hurston's 1937 novel Their Eyes Were Watching God.\nShips at a distance have every man's wish on board. For some they come in with the tide. For others they sail forever on the horizon, never out of sight, never landing.",q:"Which choice best describes the function of the ships in the text?",choices:["They describe a historical voyage.","They are a metaphor for hopes and dreams, some fulfilled and some not.","They set up a conflict between two sailors.","They explain how tides work."],ans:1,exp:"The ships carry “every man's wish”; some arrive and others never land — a metaphor for dreams fulfilled or unfulfilled."},
  {id:"r112",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"The new restaurant's menu is surprisingly ___ : despite the small kitchen, it offers dishes from more than a dozen regional cuisines.",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",choices:["limited","eclectic","expensive","predictable"],ans:1,exp:"Dishes from more than a dozen cuisines means a wide-ranging, varied menu — “eclectic.”"},
  {id:"r113",diff:"hard",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"The scientist's initial results were promising, but she was careful not to ___ them; she noted that the sample size was small and that further trials were needed before drawing firm conclusions.",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",choices:["overstate","replicate","dismiss","publish"],ans:0,exp:"Noting the small sample and need for more trials shows caution about claiming too much — not wanting to “overstate” the results."},
  {id:"r114",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"Urban heat islands form when buildings and pavement absorb sunlight and release it as heat, making cities warmer than surrounding rural areas. A study of 30 cities found that neighborhoods with more tree cover were, on average, 4°C cooler on summer afternoons than nearby neighborhoods with little tree cover.",q:"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?",choices:["Rural areas are always cooler than cities.","Tree cover is associated with cooler temperatures in urban neighborhoods.","Pavement reflects all sunlight.","Thirty cities have eliminated urban heat islands."],ans:1,exp:"The study's central finding links more tree cover to cooler neighborhoods."},
  {id:"r115",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"Linguists have noted that languages spoken in mountainous regions are more likely to include ejective consonants — sounds made with a burst of air from the throat. One researcher proposed that thinner air at high altitude makes these sounds easier to produce. Critics respond that the pattern could instead reflect the fact that related languages, which tend to share features, often cluster in the same geographic area.",q:"Which choice best describes the critics' point?",choices:["Ejective consonants do not exist.","The link between altitude and ejectives might be explained by shared ancestry among nearby languages rather than by air pressure.","Air at high altitude is not thinner.","Mountain languages have fewer sounds overall."],ans:1,exp:"The critics suggest an alternative explanation: related languages cluster together and share features, so altitude may not be the cause."},
  {id:"r116",diff:"medium",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"The following text is from Charlotte Brontë's 1847 novel Jane Eyre.\nI am no bird; and no net ensnares me: I am a free human being with an independent will.",q:"The main purpose of the text is to",choices:["describe a bird Jane has seen","assert the speaker's independence","explain how nets are used","express fear of being trapped by an animal"],ans:1,exp:"Jane declares she is “free” with an “independent will” — a forceful assertion of independence."},
  {id:"r117",diff:"easy",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"Axolotls are salamanders native to lakes near Mexico City. Unlike most salamanders, they keep their larval features, such as feathery external gills, throughout their adult lives. They are also known for their ability to regrow lost limbs.",q:"According to the text, what is one way axolotls differ from most salamanders?",choices:["They live in lakes.","They keep larval features, such as external gills, as adults.","They cannot regrow limbs.","They are found only in deserts."],ans:1,exp:"The text says “unlike most salamanders, they keep their larval features.”"},
  {id:"r118",diff:"easy",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"The committee's decision was ___ : every member voted in favor of the proposal.",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",choices:["divided","unanimous","delayed","secret"],ans:1,exp:"Every member voting the same way means the decision was “unanimous.”"},
  {id:"r119",diff:"easy",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"The Harlem Renaissance was a flourishing of African American art, music, and literature in the 1920s and 1930s. Writers such as Langston Hughes and Zora Neale Hurston, along with musicians like Duke Ellington, helped make Harlem, in New York City, a center of cultural life.",q:"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?",choices:["Duke Ellington was a writer.","The Harlem Renaissance was a period of African American cultural achievement centered in Harlem.","Harlem is located in Chicago.","The Harlem Renaissance began in the 1960s."],ans:1,exp:"The text describes the movement as a flourishing of African American culture, centered in Harlem."},
  {id:"r120",diff:"easy",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"Although the hiking trail was described as easy, the steep climbs and loose rocks made it surprisingly ___ for most of the group.",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",choices:["relaxing","strenuous","brief","scenic"],ans:1,exp:"“Although…easy” sets up a contrast; steep climbs and loose rocks make it “strenuous.”"},
  {id:"r121",diff:"easy",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"Recycling aluminum cans uses about 95% less energy than making new aluminum from raw ore. Because aluminum can be recycled repeatedly without losing quality, a recycled can may be back on store shelves in as little as 60 days.",q:"According to the text, why is recycling aluminum beneficial?",choices:["It uses much less energy than making new aluminum.","It makes aluminum stronger.","It takes several years.","It requires more raw ore."],ans:0,exp:"The text states recycling uses about 95% less energy than making new aluminum."},
  {id:"r122",diff:"easy",domain:"Craft & Structure",kind:"reading",passage:"The young pianist's performance was so ___ that the audience rose to its feet before the final note had faded.",q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",choices:["mediocre","captivating","hesitant","forgettable"],ans:1,exp:"An audience leaping to its feet suggests the performance was “captivating.”"},
  {id:"r123",diff:"medium",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"Some sea turtles return to nest on the same beach where they hatched, even after traveling thousands of miles. Researchers think the turtles may use Earth's magnetic field as a kind of map, since each stretch of coastline has a slightly different magnetic signature.",q:"Based on the text, what do researchers think helps sea turtles find their home beaches?",choices:["The smell of the ocean","Earth's magnetic field","The position of the Moon","Following other turtles"],ans:1,exp:"The text says turtles may use Earth's magnetic field as a map."},
  {id:"r124",diff:"hard",domain:"Information & Ideas",kind:"reading",passage:"A nutrition researcher claims that eating breakfast improves students' test performance. She surveyed 500 students and found that those who reported eating breakfast scored higher on average than those who did not.",q:"Which finding, if true, would most weaken the researcher's claim?",choices:["Students who ate breakfast also tended to sleep more, which is itself linked to higher scores.","The survey included students from many schools.","Most students reported eating breakfast.","Test scores were collected from official records."],ans:0,exp:"If breakfast-eaters also sleep more, sleep (not breakfast) could explain the higher scores — a confounding variable that weakens the causal claim."}
);

ACT_SCIENCE.push(
  {id:"s101",diff:"easy",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A table shows the solubility of a salt in water at different temperatures.\nTemp (°C): 20 · 40 · 60 · 80\nSolubility (g/100 mL): 34 · 40 · 46 · 52\nAs temperature increases, solubility:",choices:["decreases","increases","stays the same","increases then decreases"],ans:1,exp:"Solubility rises from 34 to 52 as temperature rises."},
  {id:"s102",diff:"medium",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A table shows the solubility of a salt in water at different temperatures.\nTemp (°C): 20 · 40 · 60 · 80\nSolubility (g/100 mL): 34 · 40 · 46 · 52\nBased on the pattern, the solubility at 100°C would most likely be about:",choices:["52 g","55 g","58 g","64 g"],ans:2,exp:"Solubility rises 6 g for every 20°C. From 80°C to 100°C: 52 + 6 = 58 g."},
  {id:"s103",diff:"medium",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"Study 1: Students placed seeds in dishes at 10°C, 20°C, and 30°C, all with equal water and light. After 7 days, they counted sprouted seeds: 4, 15, 18.\nWhat was the independent variable in Study 1?",choices:["Amount of water","Temperature","Number of sprouted seeds","Amount of light"],ans:1,exp:"The researchers deliberately changed temperature; everything else was held constant."},
  {id:"s104",diff:"medium",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"Study 1: Students placed seeds in dishes at 10°C, 20°C, and 30°C, all with equal water and light. After 7 days, they counted sprouted seeds: 4, 15, 18.\nWhich change would best test whether light affects sprouting?",choices:["Use 40°C instead of 30°C","Keep temperature at 20°C and vary the amount of light","Use more seeds at 10°C","Count seeds after 14 days"],ans:1,exp:"To test light, change only light and hold other variables (like temperature) constant."},
  {id:"s105",diff:"hard",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"A scientist measured the rate of an enzyme reaction at different pH values.\npH: 4 · 5 · 6 · 7 · 8 · 9\nRate (units/min): 2 · 6 · 11 · 14 · 9 · 3\nAt which pH is the enzyme most active?",choices:["5","6","7","9"],ans:2,exp:"The highest rate (14) occurs at pH 7."},
  {id:"s106",diff:"hard",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A scientist measured the rate of an enzyme reaction at different pH values.\npH: 4 · 5 · 6 · 7 · 8 · 9\nRate (units/min): 2 · 6 · 11 · 14 · 9 · 3\nWhich statement best describes the relationship between pH and rate?",choices:["Rate increases as pH increases.","Rate decreases as pH increases.","Rate increases up to pH 7, then decreases.","Rate does not depend on pH."],ans:2,exp:"Rate rises to a peak at pH 7, then falls — a rise-then-fall pattern."},
  {id:"s107",diff:"hard",domain:"Conflicting Viewpoints",q:"Student 1: The Moon formed when a Mars-sized body collided with early Earth, and debris from the impact clumped together.\nStudent 2: The Moon formed elsewhere in the solar system and was later captured by Earth's gravity.\nWhich finding would most support Student 1?",choices:["Moon rocks have a very different composition from Earth rocks.","Moon rocks have oxygen isotope ratios nearly identical to Earth rocks.","The Moon has no atmosphere.","The Moon is slowly moving away from Earth."],ans:1,exp:"If the Moon formed from Earth debris, its rocks should closely match Earth's, as identical isotope ratios suggest."},
  {id:"s108",diff:"hard",domain:"Conflicting Viewpoints",q:"Student 1: The Moon formed when a Mars-sized body collided with early Earth, and debris from the impact clumped together.\nStudent 2: The Moon formed elsewhere in the solar system and was later captured by Earth's gravity.\nBoth students would most likely agree that:",choices:["the Moon formed from Earth material","the Moon currently orbits Earth","the Moon formed far from Earth","no collision ever happened"],ans:1,exp:"Both explanations end with the Moon orbiting Earth; they disagree about its origin."},
  {id:"s109",diff:"easy",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A graph shows that as the mass on a spring increases from 100 g to 400 g, the spring's stretch increases from 2 cm to 8 cm in a straight line. What is the stretch with 300 g?",choices:["4 cm","5 cm","6 cm","7 cm"],ans:2,exp:"The stretch is 2 cm per 100 g, so 300 g gives 6 cm."},
  {id:"s110",diff:"medium",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"Experiment: Four identical cups of water were left in sunlight with different lids: none, plastic, foil, and glass. After 3 hours, the water lost (mL): 12, 3, 1, 4.\nWhich lid reduced evaporation the most?",choices:["None","Plastic","Foil","Glass"],ans:2,exp:"The foil-covered cup lost the least water (1 mL)."},
  {id:"s111",diff:"medium",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"Experiment: Four identical cups of water were left in sunlight with different lids: none, plastic, foil, and glass. After 3 hours, the water lost (mL): 12, 3, 1, 4.\nWhat was the purpose of the cup with no lid?",choices:["It was the control, for comparison.","It measured the temperature.","It tested a new material.","It was a mistake in the design."],ans:0,exp:"The uncovered cup is the control — a baseline to compare the lids against."},
  {id:"s112",diff:"easy",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A table lists the density of four liquids (g/mL): Water 1.00 · Oil 0.92 · Honey 1.42 · Alcohol 0.79. If all four are poured into a cylinder and do not mix, which liquid will be on the bottom?",choices:["Water","Oil","Honey","Alcohol"],ans:2,exp:"The densest liquid sinks to the bottom. Honey (1.42 g/mL) is the densest."},
  {id:"s113",diff:"medium",domain:"Data Representation",q:"A table lists the density of four liquids (g/mL): Water 1.00 · Oil 0.92 · Honey 1.42 · Alcohol 0.79. An object with a density of 0.95 g/mL is dropped in. It will float on top of which liquid?",choices:["Oil","Alcohol","Water","None of them"],ans:2,exp:"An object floats on any liquid denser than itself. In the layered column it sinks through alcohol (0.79) and oil (0.92), then floats on water (1.00)."},
  {id:"s114",diff:"hard",domain:"Research Summaries",q:"A researcher grew bacteria with different antibiotic doses.\nDose (mg): 0 · 5 · 10 · 20\nColonies: 220 · 140 · 60 · 58\nWhich conclusion is best supported?",choices:["Any dose above 0 kills all bacteria.","Increasing the dose beyond 10 mg had little additional effect.","The antibiotic has no effect.","Colonies increase with dose."],ans:1,exp:"Going from 10 to 20 mg reduced colonies only from 60 to 58 — very little extra effect."}
);
