{"id": "ae4147", "bank": "english", "test": "act", "section": "english", "domain": "Conventions of Standard English", "skill": "Boundaries, form, and usage", "diff": "hard", "q": "“The Repair Bench” — the portion marked [11] currently reads “twice, once when the instrument arrives and once a year later,” Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to Standard English?", "choices": ["twice, once when the instrument arrives and once a year later,", "twice once when the instrument arrives and once a year later", "twice; once when the instrument arrives and once a year later", "twice, once when the instrument arrives, and once a year later,"], "ans": 0, "exp": "The paired 'once...and once' items are parenthetical and take one comma on each side.", "source": "Original practice modeled on published test skills", "dw": ["This version introduces an error in punctuation, agreement, or idiom that the explanation names.", "This version introduces an error in punctuation, agreement, or idiom that the explanation names.", "This version introduces an error in punctuation, agreement, or idiom that the explanation names."], "kind": "acteng", "passage": "In a workshop that repairs stringed instruments, [1] the most experienced technician, keeps a notebook of failures. [2] Each entry records an instrument's symptom, the repair attempted, and the outcome. [3] The notebook's purpose is not nostalgia, it is pattern recognition. [4] Over years, the technician noticed that certain cracks return after standard glue repairs, [5] especially in instruments that are stored dry. [6] Humidity, rather than the repair itself, appears to determine whether the fix holds. [7] The shop now lends every customer a small hygrometer, and [8] asks them to keep the instrument between forty and sixty percent humidity. [9] Returns for repeat cracks have fallen, though the technician warns that the sample is small and the shop's climate, [10] unusually stable. [11] She also photographs each repair twice, once when the instrument arrives and once a year later, because photographs preserve details that memory smooths away. [12] The notebook and the photographs together make the case that repairs fail for reasons a careful record can reveal.", "marker": "[11]"},
{"id": "ae4148", "bank": "english", "test": "act", "section": "english", "domain": "Conventions of Standard English", "skill": "Boundaries, form, and usage", "diff": "easy", "q": "“The Repair Bench” — the portion marked [12] currently reads “make the case that repairs fail for reasons a careful record can reveal.” Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to Standard English?", "choices": ["make the case that repairs fail, for reasons a careful record can reveal.", "make the case that repairs fail for reasons, a careful record can reveal.", "make the case, that repairs fail for reasons a careful record can reveal.", "make the case that repairs fail for reasons a careful record can reveal."], "ans": 3, "exp": "'Make the case that' takes an object clause with no internal punctuation.", "source": "Original practice modeled on published test skills", "dw": ["This version introduces an error in punctuation, agreement, or idiom that the explanation names.", "This version introduces an error in punctuation, agreement, or idiom that the explanation names.", "This version introduces an error in punctuation, agreement, or idiom that the explanation names."], "kind": "acteng", "passage": "In a workshop that repairs stringed instruments, [1] the most experienced technician, keeps a notebook of failures. [2] Each entry records an instrument's symptom, the repair attempted, and the outcome. [3] The notebook's purpose is not nostalgia, it is pattern recognition. [4] Over years, the technician noticed that certain cracks return after standard glue repairs, [5] especially in instruments that are stored dry. [6] Humidity, rather than the repair itself, appears to determine whether the fix holds. [7] The shop now lends every customer a small hygrometer, and [8] asks them to keep the instrument between forty and sixty percent humidity. [9] Returns for repeat cracks have fallen, though the technician warns that the sample is small and the shop's climate, [10] unusually stable. [11] She also photographs each repair twice, once when the instrument arrives and once a year later, because photographs preserve details that memory smooths away. [12] The notebook and the photographs together make the case that repairs fail for reasons a careful record can reveal.", "marker": "[12]"}
